## Round-42 additions — single-run receipts, per-correction evidence, whole-token reads and defeat-shaped mutants (append-only)

R6 form: every line above keeps its number — this block is appended after line 337 (the file is
60663 bytes before it, `head -c 60663 corpus/QUALITY.md | sha256sum` →
`a76f42d76f10c123be853f0575bb347848d38e7dcdabdcb2d4eaaccb32a19ac6`; `wc -l` → 337), and R22 (line 226),
R23 (line 227), R24 (line 228), R25 (line 229), R26 (line 249), R27 (line 250), R28 (line 263), R29
(line 264), R30 (line 277), R31 (line 278), R32 (line 291), R33 (line 292), R34 (line 307), R35 (line 308),
R36 (line 309), R37 (line 323) and R38 (line 337) are not edited. The landing commit re-runs both commands
immediately before writing: a byte figure measured at draft time is a stale premise under R19 if an earlier
append in the same round moved it.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R39 | A receipt — a round's `final-gates/` transcript, or any file a lane record cites as the output of a run — is only ever the unedited tee of one observed run: one invocation writes it once and nothing appends to it or edits it afterwards, so its bytes are that invocation's stdout+stderr in the order printed; a receipt composed of two runs, one whose header line its instrument does not print, one whose tally was amended after the fact, or one carrying a value its instrument never emitted is not a receipt, and the result it carried is discarded while the claim it served reads `report-only`. The receipt's `run-utc` is printed by the run itself at execution, so a value copied from the commit under test is a label for that commit and not an execution instant: a receipt whose only instant equals the named ref's committer instant (`time-basis=ref-commit`, the r41 shape) states when the ref was committed and nothing about when the suite ran, and it prints the execution instant separately or the record labels its timing `report-only`. Two `run-utc=` lines, a `run-utc` later than the containing commit (R37), or a receipt naming a head its own export did not contain make it a forged record under R37: discarded, never promoted or relabelled. | R37 (`:323`) carries the tee clause inside its record-quoting rule; R39 is the receipt-side specialization and leaves R37's transcript-or-`report-only` limb unchanged. The historical mint is M39-2 (`corpus/lessons/round-39.md:77`): a committed header `make-receipt.sh` never printed. The surviving gap is measured in the r41 receipt, `.ufo/waves/round-41/final-gates/all-gates.txt` (31 lines, sha256 `0915f51d774e5f4bd744912b6b2a87b7387f5437730bef166457766ca10b9e94`): `export-ref=1f7464612ac4c44e39886b82e812610d3c039b13` at `:2`, `time-basis=ref-commit` at `:4`, `run-utc=2026-10-27T12:16:34Z` at `:5`, and that instant is exactly J's committer time (`git show -s --format=%ct 1f746461` → 1793103394 → `2026-10-27T12:16:34Z`), so the file's only instant is the ref's; the r41 verifier's step-1 table read it `= J committer instant … conformant` and `SCORECARD.md:1605` writes it as '`run-utc` equal to the ref's committer instant'. Nothing in the receipt evidences a single invocation or when it ran. Round-42 limbs: J8 re-derives each payload value from the transcript rather than trusting the receipt's tally and rejects a stitched, hand-headed or ref-timed receipt; the r42 suite is authored this round, so J8 is a landing requirement, not a draft measurement. |
| R40 | Evidence that a correction landed is per correction and never a union aggregate: each `Correction (round N)` line, or the block that line opens, is graded on its own bytes for its own firing-fixture paths, its own kill-rule clause and its own corrected values, and the check prints one line per correction — its id, its own verdict and the identifiers it fails to name — so a reader sees which correction is unsupported. A count over the union satisfies nothing: `grep -c` over three records printing 8 and 3 and 3 proves eight and three and three lines, not eight and three and three corrections, and 'the three records' correction blocks collectively name X, Y and Z' or 'each meets >=1' grades no single correction. A duplicated block is one correction repeated and is recorded once, with the duplicate counted as the defect it is (D-r41-1's shape), never as coverage; a file whose first qualified line supplies the identifiers for the unqualified lines after it has graded one line, not the file. Per-correction evidence is the unit; a union tally of qualified lines, stems or occurrences is not. | M42-1 (`corpus/lessons/round-42.md` § Mistakes, minted this round — the evidence-union class) and the r41 I1 defeat it derives from. Committed: `wave2/CritC1KillRule.md:18` `selftest detail=I1 mutant=evidence-not-named rejected=0 rc=0 payload=records=3,lines=14,graded=3,evidence=3/3,missing=0` — a tree whose named identifiers were removed still graded `evidence=3/3`; and `wave2/RevCorrectionsFinal.md:5-6` reads the union in the lane's own words, '`SCORECARD.md` **8**, `LOG.md` **3**, `SHONEN.md` **3** (each meets >=1)' and 'The three records' correction blocks collectively name …'. The instrument's I1 is that aggregate: `corpus/instruments/r41-closure.sh:891-914` counts `graded` once per file and `ev` once per evidence stem over the union (`:902-907`, the `seen` dedupe), so three corrections in one file are supported by one qualified line. The duplication that exposes it is D-r41-1 (`corpus/lessons/round-41-verifier-spotcheck.md:132-134`): `SCORECARD.md:1602-1605` and `:1607-1610` are byte-identical (measured: `diff` of the two spans exits 0). R17/R24 grade an instrument's limb and a selftest case line, never the evidence inside a correction, so the row is new. Round-42 limbs: J1 grades each correction; J11 rejects a duplicated Correction block; both replay the committed defeat artifacts. |
| R41 | A counted or compared value is read as a whole token and the counter prints the token it read. A matcher for `closed=7/8` rejects `closed=7/80`; one for `failures=1` rejects `failures=10`; a lookup for row `S064` never matches `S0641` or `S0640`, and a status read keys on the whole id cell, not a substring of it. A value that occurs only inside quoted defect evidence — `closed=7/80` and `failures=10` inside a correction block that quotes an attack receipt — is not a result value and is never counted as one. The matcher anchors both ends of the literal (a non-word character or string end on each side, or an exact-line match), and the payload names the token it matched and the near-token it rejected, so a prefix-only match is visible in the receipt instead of inferred from a clean total; a payload that prints only `tally=3/3` without the token read cannot be graded under this row. | M42-2 (the round's token-boundary `## Mistakes` row) and the round-40 T2 class it completes: `SCORECARD.md:1602` states T2 as '`closed=7/80` and `failures=10` accepted as green by substring'. The r41 instrument fixes the matcher in `value_present` (`corpus/instruments/r41-closure.sh:443-459`: 'the characters around the occurrence must be whitespace or punctuation'), and a scratch replay of that function measured the matcher correct (`closed=7/80` vs `closed=7/8` → reject, `failures=10` vs `failures=1` → reject, `closed=10/10` → accept; commands in `.ufo/waves/round-42/wave1/ScribeQuality.md`), yet round 41's I3 payload reads `tally=3/3,mangled=0` (`wave2/RevC1Counters.md:24`, `wave2/CritC1KillRule.md:20`) beside a mutant writing `closed=10/100` (`r41-closure.sh:1757-1758`) — the mangled token was never the only carrier, because `replace_line` (`:1683-1690`, the `!done` guard) rewrites one line and `SCORECARD.md` carries eight correction lines of which two hold bounded `closed=10/10` (`1603`, `1608`; scratch replay: `bounded_truth_hits_before=2`, after replace-first `2`, after replace-all `0`). R3 names a matcher; this row fixes what a matcher is allowed to read and what its payload must show. The quoted-defect limb is committed at `wave2/RevCorrectionsFinal.md:8`, both near-tokens in three correction lines 'solely as quoted T2 defect/false-green evidence'. Round-42 limbs: J2 grades each token and replays the mangled-token defeat. |
| R42 | A check counts as repaired only when its selftest's mutant set contains the committed defeat shape for its family — the input a committed attack receipt or routed defect filed, at the bytes it filed them, not a stand-in the author composed — and that mutant is executed against the current instrument bytes and rejected (`mutant=<name> rejected=1 rc=1`, recorded in the selftest line). A family whose committed defeat is absent from the mutant set, or whose mutant greens (`rejected=0 rc=0`) on a tree carrying the family's committed defect, leaves the check unrepaired whatever its positive case prints; a mutant that rewrites one occurrence while the committed defect is a duplicated block mutates nothing, because the shape, not the string, is the defeat. Each selftest prints one graded line per case with its mutant count and verdict (R24's shape: `selftest case=<id> mutants=<k>/<n> … verdict=pass`), and where the committed artifact lives outside the suite — a wave-2 receipt, a probe transcript — the suite carries it as a replay fixture bound by path and digest or re-executes the artifact itself, never a paraphrase of it. | The r41 failure this row generalizes: `wave2/CritC1KillRule.md:18,20` record `mutant=evidence-not-named rejected=0 rc=0` and `mutant=mangled-value-token rejected=0 rc=0`, with `:21-22` `selftest case=I1 mutants=1/2 … verdict=fail` and `selftest case=I3 mutants=0/1 … verdict=fail`, and the receipt repeats them (`round-41/final-gates/all-gates.txt:18,20`) — both mutants name the right class and neither reproduces the committed defeat, because `replace_line` (`r41-closure.sh:1683-1690`, `!done`) rewrites one line per file while the corrected records carry duplicated blocks (`SCORECARD.md:1602-1605,1607-1610`, byte-identical), so the mutated tree keeps an unmutated qualified line (scratch replay: bounded hits 2 before, 2 after replace-first, 0 after replace-all). The family list is the round-42 claim's seven — F-EVID, F-TOKEN, F-TABLE, F-DECL, F-SCAN, F-BIND, F-FLIP — whose kill rule reads 'the committed replay limb of any J1-J7 check is absent or skipped (a defeat that is not replayed is not repaired)' (`.ufo/waves/round-42/claims.json` C1 kill_rule), and the blind spot the r41 verifier left open is a family instance: 'the frontier counter's status-flip blind spot (26/26 exit 0 without the explicit F24 PASS line)' (`corpus/lessons/round-41-verifier-spotcheck.md:148-149`), probed by `wave2/MutFrontierParser.md`. R17 kills a limb with a one-limb mutant and R24 requires a graded case line; neither requires the mutant set to carry the committed defeat, which is how the r41 rows stayed green. Round-42 limbs: J1-J7 each carry a REPLAY limb over the committed artifact. |

