| R43 | A committed record that reports a run — a `Correction (round N)` cell, a LOG bullet, a lesson paragraph, a frozen claim's `before` cell — is graded by a committed counter over that record's own bytes, never by its prose: a `corrections=<n>` literal must equal what the suite re-derives at the receipt it cites (the r43 mine: `SCORECARD.md:1634` states `J11 payload=corrections=33` while `final-gates/all-gates.txt:16` and the r42 engine's own `check_j11` re-derivation over the same export print `corrections=34`, which K14 reads as `K14 payload=literals=2,stale=1,superseded=0,live=corrections=34,groups=4 closed=0` at base); a sentence naming a `kasegi-rNN-*` wave as dispatched needs `.ufo/waves/round-NN/<wave>-results.json` (the r43 mine: `LOG.md:1804` and `corpus/lessons/round-42.md:11,16` count 36 wave-2 seats and a 100-seat round total for a wave that was handed off and never taken — no `.ready.dispatched`, no results file); a `## ` section may not promise its own verdicts for a report that does not exist (the r43 mine: `corpus/lessons/round-42.md:52` `## Wave-2 falsification` says "Appended when the round-42 falsify wave reports"); a quoted `closed=`/`failures=` value must appear in a committed transcript at the ref it names (the r43 mine: `closed=10/13`, `failures=3` at `de09313` quoted in `corpus/lessons/round-42-verifier-spotcheck.md:65`, while the committed red transcript `final-gates/all-gates.pre-repair.txt:19,33` at `d238e36` prints `closed=9/13 exit=1`, `selftest cases=13 failures=4`); and a frozen claim's `before` cell must be the literal its own cited control printed (the r43 mine: round-42 C1 `before` says `closed=10/12 exit=1` while the control it cites, `.ufo/waves/round-42/c1-controls/do-nothing.txt:16`, prints `closed=8/12 exit=1`; the round-43 predicate draft that pre-declared `closed=13/19` before its control existed was withdrawn under the same reading, `M43-1`). | RD42-1, RD42-2, RD42-3, RD42-4, RD42-5, RD42-8 and M43-1 — `corpus/lessons/round-43.md` §Mistakes and `.ufo/waves/round-43/c1-controls/do-nothing.txt` §C; machine-graded by K14-K18 of `corpus/instruments/r43-closure.sh` (`w1/ExecK14Corrections.transcript.txt` §1-§7, `w1/ExecK16Placeholder.transcript.txt`, `w1/ExecK17ClaimsBefore.sh`) |
| R44 | A claim's declared freeze instant is corroborated by the artifacts that carry it, never believed as a label: the freeze record states `frozen-at` as an ISO-8601 `Z` instant that is not earlier than the latest `measured-at` among the controls it cites and not later than the commit that contains it, its `claims-sha256` equals the claims file's bytes, and a label later than its own containing commit (the r43 mine: round-42 `claims.json` `frozen_at_utc=2026-10-28T12:05:00Z` against commit `0566bd66`'s committer stamp `2026-10-28 08:00:01 -0300` = `11:00:01Z` and file mtime `10:51:52Z`, a 65-minute future label, `corpus/lessons/round-42-verifier-spotcheck.md:178`) reads as no freeze record — K19 at base prints `K19 payload=freeze=absent,order=bad,sha=mismatch closed=0`, and an offset form or a second, later `measured-at` in a cited control reds the same way. | RD42-6 — `corpus/lessons/round-43.md` §Mistakes, `corpus/lessons/round-42-verifier-spotcheck.md:38,178`, round-42 `wave1/ConnCrossClaims.md:54` (H12); machine-graded by K19 of `corpus/instruments/r43-closure.sh` (`w1/ExecK19Freeze.transcript.txt`: live `K19 payload=freeze=present,order=ok,sha=match closed=1`; mutants `m-early`, `m-offset`, `m-dupe-measured`, `m-max-drift` each `order=bad closed=0`) |
| R45 | A capability row enters `SCORECARD.md` only with the demonstration command and the output that command actually printed in the same round: the row's evidence pointer is an executable command, the round's receipt holds its output, and until that run exists the cell reads `<pending: demo>` and no row enters — a capability, a measure or a rate stated without the command that produced it is a claim about a run, not a capability (the r43 mine: round-42's C2 after-state quoted `measure 45/45 = 1.000`, which round-42's own critic recorded as unexercised — `wave1/RevC2Shape.md:152`: "No measure (45/45 = 1.000) command was exercised: it is not in the nine counting commands and its tool is not named"; round 43's `claim-package-gate` row therefore ships `<pending: demo>` and names `python3 corpus/instruments/claim-package-check.py corpus/instruments/fixtures/claim-package/exemplar` as the command that admits it). | round-42 `wave1/RevC2Shape.md:152`, quoted by `.ufo/waves/round-43/w1/ScribeReportSkeleton.report.md`; `w1/ScribeScorecardRound43.append.md` §Round-43 capabilities (`<pending: demo>`); C2 of `.ufo/waves/round-43/claims.json` |
| R46 | An exemplar's `claimable` state is the operator's to set, never the gate's to infer: a package gate reads `claimable` only as the package's own declared queue bit, checks that it is a JSON boolean consistent with the package's gates (`true` with a failing hard gate or subcheck is DEFECT; `false` with a named blocker is well-formed), and never requires `claimable === true` as a condition of evidence, never raises or clears it from a clean rule pass, and never writes it — importing the lost checker's `claimable === true` requirement makes the round's own exemplar DEFECT and fails the frozen claim (the r43 mine: `w1/ScoutClaimContract.md:149`, "That single import makes the exemplar DEFECT and fails the frozen claim", against `claim-gates.md:68`; the contract's own census has `claimable` absent 241/280 and true 12/280). | `w1/ScoutClaimContract.md:149,183-188`, `w1/ExecGateChain.md:108-109,130-131` (mutations `claimable-true-failing-gate` and `claimable-false-no-blocker` both graded `verdict=pass`; probes `claimable=true finding_count=0 rejected ok=yes` and `claimable=false with named blocker accepted ok=yes`); C2 of `.ufo/waves/round-43/claims.json` ("`claimable` stays false and the operator gates are named in `claim_checklist.md`") |

## Round-45 additions - do-nothing replay before data, presence-only limbs and capture-method parity (append-only)

R6 form: every line above keeps its number — this block is appended after line 372 (the file is 78651 bytes before it, `head -c 78651 corpus/QUALITY.md | sha256sum` → `dd753497f7138c395230df48443d3d356ca0d67c3d8a8cd24d9989103dd43d03`; `wc -l` → 372), and R22 (line 226), R23 (line 227), R24 (line 228), R25 (line 229), R26 (line 249), R27 (line 250), R28 (line 263), R29 (line 264), R30 (line 277), R31 (line 278), R32 (line 291), R33 (line 292), R34 (line 307), R35 (line 308), R36 (line 309), R37 (line 323), R38 (line 337), R39 (line 351), R40 (line 352), R41 (line 353), R42 (line 354), R43 (line 369), R44 (line 370), R45 (line 371) and R46 (line 372) are not edited. The landing commit re-runs both commands immediately before writing: a byte figure measured at draft time is a stale premise under R19 if an earlier append in the same round moved it.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R47 | A frozen counter is replayed on the cheapest do-nothing control (the unmodified base tree, or the tree with the claimed change removed) and must print its declared before-value BEFORE data: a counter that prints `0` where the declared before is nonzero is unmeasurable, so the claim it grades stays unfrozen until the replay prints that value. Narrower than R22 and R34 (satisfiability and dry-run over the predicate's constants): R47 binds the declared before-VALUE, read as a whole line, and is mechanised by `corpus/instruments/freeze-check.py`, which replays each frozen counter at the base and fails with `FC-NO-MATCH` when the line is absent or `FC-AFTER-AT-BASE` when the do-nothing tree already reads the after-value. The ordering (before any seat scores) is a freeze-record duty (R44), not something the tool can see. The r44 mine: the frozen C2 grep printed `0` at base and `0` after the admission while the table-aware count read `rows=25 admitted=25` (`corpus/lessons/round-44.md:15`, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:126`; `.ufo/waves/round-44/controls/c2-frozen-counter-replay.txt`). enforced-by: `corpus/instruments/freeze-check.py` | `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:15`, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:126` (M44-3, M44-8), `corpus/lessons/round-44-verifier-spotcheck.md` §6 item 4; C1 of `.ufo/waves/round-45/claims.json` |
| R48 | A limb that grades a committed artifact is presence-only when it only checks that a file, heading, row or word exists; a presence-only predicate accepts denials, stubs and fabricated trees, so every limb binds content: pinned bytes by sha256, located quotes at their anchors, affirmative tokens read as whole values, rows read only from the real table (not a fence or a comment). Each limb is graded by mutants that keep the prose marker and remove or fake the content, and a mutant that survives is a defect of the limb, not of the mutant (the r44 mines: a marker-only tree graded `fixed=8`, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:16`; a fully fabricated evidence tree graded `fixed=8 unresolved=0`, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:189`; presence-only predicates accepted a denial and a stub emitter, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:125`; the r45 wave-1 mines: `flip: tbd`, a row inside a fence, a stub that prints the selftest tokens). enforced-by: `corpus/instruments/r45-counters-selftest-c1.py` | `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:16`, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:125`, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:189` (M44-4, M44-7, M44-10), `corpus/lessons/round-44-verifier-spotcheck.md` §6 item 2; `.ufo/waves/round-45/wave1/R45RevLimbR1.md` |
| R49 | The capture method used to ADMIT a source is also the capture method used to REJECT one: a reject reason is graded only on text read through the admitting method, so a plain fetch that returns an application shell supports no reject reason about the document's content when the admit read the same document through a first-party API; a reason the second capture contradicts is corrected by a new `supersedes` row and an appended lesson correction, not left standing (the r44 mine: S070's reason was read from the shell page while the S069 admit used the article API, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:22` and `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:131`; the readable body was 10029 characters). The tool side is partial: `corpus/instruments/source-recheck.py` re-verifies a pinned capture's sha256 and quotes, and `corpus/instruments/capture-binding-check.py` forbids a rejected row anchoring a corpus manifest record (rule B3), so a rejected row's capture lives beside the wave evidence with its own manifest; the parity itself is a review duty checked by the non-author spot-check. enforced-by: `corpus/instruments/source-recheck.py` | `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:22`, `corpus/lessons/round-44.md:131` (S070 reject), `corpus/lessons/round-44-verifier-spotcheck.md` §4 S070 row and §6 item 1; `corpus/SOURCES.md` S072 |

## Round-46 additions - freeze-ready counters, routed-defect re-derivation and per-tree plain measures (append-only)

R6 form: every line above keeps its number; this block is appended after the round-45 block (R47-R49 are not edited). Sources: `corpus/lessons/round-46.md` § Mistakes (M46-1, M46-2, M46-3), `.ufo/waves/round-46/counter-errata.md`, `.ufo/waves/round-46/controls/controls-v1.2.out.txt`.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R50 | A counter is freeze-ready only after its author has run a single-edit mutant battery against it BEFORE the freeze: at least three mutants per limb, each leaving the prose marker or the claimed text in place while breaking the bound content, and every survivor either closed in the counter or named as a disclosed mechanical limit in the claim. R48 already requires limbs to bind content; R50 adds the ordering and the count, because the round-46 C1 counter v1 was frozen with nine cheap controls and then read 7/7 on eleven trees that carried the defect (a comment-trick recheck line, a `cd ~/...` line, a shadow line, a planted anchor, a dropped restoration line, a hostile line, three C2 filler shapes and more), found only by the wave-1 falsifiers. enforced-by: `corpus/instruments/r46-counters-selftest-c1.py` and `r46-counters-selftest-c2.py` (a battery that prints `selftest cases=<N> failures=<F>` and is run against the frozen counter with `--counter` to show it can fail) and the red-before columns of `.ufo/waves/round-46/controls/controls-v1.2.out.txt` (eleven controls green under the frozen v1, red under the hardened counters). | M46-1 (class `vacuous`; captured-by R48, which existed and was not applied before the freeze); `wave1/R46RevC1L3.md`, `wave1/R46MutC2a.md`, `wave2/R46W2ExecControlsV11.md` |
| R51 | A routed defect is re-derived from committed bytes or clocks before a gate is built on it. A gate whose only motivating case is a reviewer's reading of a timestamp is first run on a pair that is in order by every existence clock (the reflog of both repositories, the committer times, a constructed honest rewrite) and must pass it; a check that fails every honest history and cannot separate it from the defect is withdrawn, not shipped. The round-46 landing gate first carried `date-order-author`; it failed the round-44, round-28 and round-45 pairs and a constructed honest amend alike (the author time of a report commit survives an amend or a jj rewrite, the committer time does not), so the check was removed and the spot-check item recorded as not reproduced by the committed clocks. enforced-by: `corpus/instruments/r46-landing-gate.py` selftest case `post-honest-rewrite-author-earlier-than-cited-round45-shape` (must PASS) beside `post-date-inversion-committer-and-author` (must FAIL). | M46-2 (class `stale-premise`; captured-by none); `wave2/R46W2CritMistake.md` F1-F4 |
| R52 | A re-ground that un-gates a recheck line keeps the old line (as `restoration-recheck:`), names one committed anchor per current claim over at least two files, says in the entry which claims stay historical and that a committed re-implementation written from the entry proves the entry's description is implemented, not that the lost tool behaved so; and each claim carries a plain second measurement (grep, wc or awk over committed bytes, no python counter) with its before-value stated per start tree, the base and the freeze commit. A counter reading 7/7 is not support for the rows' faithfulness: the side-by-side reading of each row against its anchored lines is, and the claim names who made it. enforced-by: `corpus/instruments/r46-c1-gate.py` limbs L3-L6 and `.ufo/waves/round-46/controls/plain-rows.sh` (the fixed-string re-derivation), with the hollow-rows control `c1 hollow-rows` printed GREEN in `controls-v1.2.out.txt` as the disclosed limit. | M46-3 (class `scope`; captured-by Q4 and R51 not applicable); `wave1/R46Revenger.md`, `wave1/R46CritSoftening.md`, `wave2/R46W2RevGround*.md` |
| R53 | A counter's pre-freeze falsification runs BOTH attack classes: single-edit mutants of the counter (R50) AND tree-level false-green probes per limb — trees that keep every marker the limb checks and remove or disguise the bound content (indented or commented duplicate rows, substring-aliased cells, redirector-wrapped URLs, wrong-file locators, mid-line-comment quotes, duplicated section headings a downstream validator ignores) — and every such tree must read red at the freeze bytes, because a mutant pass alone proves the counter rejects ITS OWN mutations, never that it rejects a defective world (the r46-attempt-2 mine: the f29 gate froze with an 11-flip R50 receipt and zero tree probes, and the wave-1 reviewers found seven tree-level false-greens — `wave1/R46bRevF29H1.md` F1/F3, `R46bRevF29H2H3.md` F1/F3/F5, `R46bRevF29H4H5.md` F2, `R46bRevF29H6H7.md` F5/F6 — every one closed in gate v1.1, `attempt2/counter-errata.md` E4). enforced-by: the fixture builders committed beside the counter (`controls/f29-fixture.py`, `controls/f29-defects.py`) are the tree-probe harness; a freeze without them is not freeze-ready.

## Round-47 addition — a repair cannot green one gate by breaking another

All existing criteria remain unchanged. This block is grounded in `corpus/lessons/round-47.md` M47-3 and `.ufo/waves/round-47/w1/R47RevD7.md`.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R54 | A superseding source row does not automatically reject its predecessor. Before changing a captured row to rejected, run the retained capture-binding check on a scratch before/after pair. If rejection creates `rejected-row-anchor`, preserve the historical mined row and its pinned evidence, change only permitted used-by/status/recheck cells as justified, and log old→new. If a frozen claim demanded the harmful transition, report that claim unmet; do not weaken B3 or rewrite the frozen predicate to manufacture success. | M47-3; the independent D7 probe discriminated `records=3 bound=3` from `bound=2 unbound=1` by changing only S074's status; `.ufo/waves/round-47/w1/R47RevD7.receipts.txt`. |

## Round-48 addition — count the intended rendered record

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R55 | A plain expression used as a frozen row target anchors the table-row shape and whole identifier cells, not a phrase also found in prose. Enumerate the matches before freezing. A checklist is its canonical seed plus permitted marks and one evidence line per box; historical assessments belong in reports, not appended replacement checklists. | `corpus/lessons/round-48.md` M48-1/M48-2; round-47 non-author spot-check R1/R2. The actual drift fixture and phrase-versus-row discrimination are retained under `.ufo/waves/round-48/controls/`. |

## Round-48 attempt-2 addition — catch what the attempt-1 gates missed

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R56 | A Gate's stated word count for a located quote comes from `wc -w` on the quoted text, run again at each round that cites it. A hand-typed count is a claim to re-measure, not evidence. | `corpus/lessons/round-48.md` M48-3 (16 claimed, 14 measured). |
| R57 | A frozen battery's positive control is the byte sequence the claim actually ships, not a pre-freeze draft. If the shipped bytes differ from every fixture, add a non-frozen fixture for them before scoring; never amend the frozen fixture file. | `corpus/lessons/round-48.md` M48-4; wave-1 `R48Review31.md` findings 1-2. |
| R58 | A lowest-axis claim must move a metric that the axis counter itself reads. A claim that changes only a file the axis census never reads is labelled nominal and its score is held, with the non-moving metric named. | `corpus/lessons/round-48.md` M48-5; wave-1 `R48Review28.md` section 1. |
| R59 | A counter with a documented verdict map must return the mapped exit for unreadable input, including NUL bytes and special filenames. An uncaught exception that exits 1 is a verdict-map violation even when it fails closed. | `corpus/lessons/round-48.md` M48-6; wave-1 `R48Review17.md` observation 1 (case 28). |
## Round-49 addition — bases, fixtures and the verdict map (append-only)

All existing criteria remain unchanged. This block is grounded in the round-49 lesson
(`corpus/lessons/round-49.md` § Mistakes) and in the round-48 non-author closure's routed defects.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R60 | A frozen fixture battery's positive control is the byte sequence the claim SHIPS. When it is not, the fix is a new NON-frozen fixture file carrying the shipped bytes (pinned by digest) plus the shapes the battery does not cover; the frozen fixture file is never amended after scoring. | `corpus/lessons/round-49.md` § Mistakes (the M48-4 row) and the round-48 closure's routed defect 1; landed as `corpus/instruments/shonen-shipped-fixtures.json` with the runner `corpus/instruments/shonen-shipped-fixture-check.py` (`selftest cases=5 failures=0`), which pins the shipped SHONEN.md bytes (sha256 `b1352168…`) as the positive control and covers the unchecked-box-with-evidence shape the round-48 battery did not cover. |
| R61 | A counter with a documented verdict map maps unreadable input to its mapped exit, and the amendment that does so is disclosed with the frozen-claim evidence preserved: the amendment touches only the unreadable-input path, the frozen claim's measurement never exercised that path, and the claim's own self-test still reads the same case tally. | `corpus/lessons/round-49.md` § Mistakes (the M48-6 row) and the round-48 closure's routed defect 2; landed as the `ValueError` arm in `corpus/instruments/shonen-form.py` (`SF-UNREADABLE` → exit 2) with the probe `corpus/instruments/shonen-shipped-fixture-check.py` case `nul-in-evidence-pointer` and the round-48 self-test re-run at `cases=17 failures=0`. |
| R62 | A base repair that changes only the PATH PREFIX of a cite, keeps every line anchor and every sentence, and records the referent reasoning per row is not the rewrite the legacy-cite rule forbids; a repair that moves a line number, or points a cite at a different file with the same name, is refused. The ledger is the receipt. | `corpus/lessons/round-49.md` § Mistakes (the rule-6 tension row) and `corpus/knowledge/citation-bases.md` § "Rule 6, round-49 amendment"; the repair is 68 rows in `corpus/instruments/citation-reground-ledger.md`, each with its sites and its referent note. |
| R63 | The lane's citation vocabulary must name every base the lane cites. A citation written against a base the vocabulary does not define is a defect of the vocabulary, not of the citation: the base is added with its repair rule, and the citations that named it are repaired to that base. | `corpus/lessons/round-49.md` § Mistakes (the missing FLEET base row) and `corpus/knowledge/citation-bases.md` § Base names (the FLEET entry); the repaired class is 11 fleet-root-relative citations, all recorded in the ledger. |
| R64 | A source that was OPENED and then refused is a registry row with status `rejected`, its reason and its flip condition — never a search-log sentence alone, and never a report line that says no row was rejected. | the round-48 non-author closure's routed curation defect 4 (`corpus/lessons/round-48-verifier-spotcheck.md` §5 item 4); landed as the rejected row S079 (Bugcrowd researcher submission), whose `used-by` cell is `—` because no admitted entry cites it. |

## Round-57 additions — host-portable derives and capture-method disclosure (append-only)

R6 form: every line above keeps its number; this block is appended after line 428 (R64). The two rows close the round-57 Mistakes rows whose `captured-by` cell was `none` (R36): M57-1 and M57-2 in `corpus/lessons/round-57.md`.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R65 | A figure a committed record states by re-deriving it is host-portable only when its derive reads committed bytes: a rev-pinned `git show` or `git ls-tree`, or a receipt line the record itself cites. A derive that re-runs a live counter whose result depends on the host (a cd-prefixed recheck that resolves on one machine only, a Windows-only FAIL class) is host-bound: the record names the host it was measured on, and the do-nothing receipt on the running host must reproduce the recorded figure before the derive is cited as evidence. | M57-1 (`corpus/lessons/round-57.md` § Mistakes): the r53 `fail-entry-records` derive re-ran the frontier counter; on the run-15 Linux host the counter reads `problem-rows=0`, so the audit read `figures=27 ok=26 stale=1 exit=1` at the base bytes (`.ufo/waves/round-57/controls/do-nothing-c1-r57.txt`). Repaired to read the cited receipt line `FAIL_entry_records=33` (`.ufo/waves/round-53/controls/c2-counter-final-r53.txt:8`); the repaired derive can fail, shown by the red control `red2-deriverepair-r57.json` (derived=34 want=33, `.ufo/waves/round-57/controls/c1-red-r57.txt`). |
| R66 | When a host refuses a committed capture instrument's own open (an HTTP error on the instrument's default request), the workaround is disclosed where the evidence is read: the manifest note, the failing invocation's error line, the public-URL request that succeeds (here curl, a different TLS client stack, with the instrument's own User-Agent string), and the instrument's own conversion reused unmodified. The mechanism is named by a controlled probe, not assumed from the status code: which request element flips the outcome (a header, the User-Agent, or the TLS client). The admitting round's Gate states the capture method; a non-author verifier re-opens with an independent conversion and compares the text, never only the sha. | M57-2 (`corpus/lessons/round-57.md` § Mistakes): `capture-source.py --id PROBE --url https://www.iso.org/standard/72311.html` exits 1 with HTTP Error 403 in 5/5 runs at this host while curl gets 200. Seat Revenge403 (`.ufo/waves/round-57/w1/Revenge403.md` §1–§5): the 403 is a Cloudflare managed challenge; no header or User-Agent variant discriminates; the discriminator is the TLS ClientHello that CPython's `http.client` emits (ALPN and PHA, `/usr/lib/python3.14/http/client.py:845-855`). The row closes the `captured-by: none` gap; the capture-binding and source-recheck limbs remain the mechanical check. |
| R67 | A frozen counter that admits an entry grades the entry's substance, not only its structure: a stub body that keeps the header lines and names the rechecked ids in prose must not read as an admitted entry. Until the counter enforces this, a round's admission rests on the non-author claim table (every load-bearing sentence against its committed snapshot, the Gate's word counts), and the round states that the counter is non-discriminating for body substance. Closure check (open until it prints `closed`): the stub probe `(a2)` of the round-57 CriticFrontier seat — a copy of the entry body replaced by one stub line naming `S089` and `S090` — must make `frontier-recheck-count.py --root <probe>` exit 1. | M57-4 (`corpus/lessons/round-57.md` § Mistakes): the frozen C2 counter's citation limb (`corpus/instruments/frontier-recheck-count.py:260-261`) accepts any bare S-id token in prose as citation; probe (a2) PASSES (`.ufo/waves/round-57/w1/CriticFrontier.md`). Captured-by: none in the counter; the non-author table is the capture. Named open; repair is a frozen-counter amendment in a later round (no amendment this round: the predicate was frozen before the probe). |
| R68 | A figure-audit derive that pipes a git read into a counter must fail closed when the object is absent: the object's existence is checked first (`git cat-file -e <rev>:<path> && …`), because a pipeline whose left side dies still prints a zero that matches a zero want. The row closes the R30 defect in two round-54 figures (`figure-audit-mentions-base-log`, `figure-audit-mentions-base-knowledge-expected`), repaired in round 57; a missing-object red control reads stale (`.ufo/waves/round-57/controls/r30-hardening-r57.txt`: `derived=UNAVAILABLE reason=derive-exit-128 stale`, exit 1). | M57-5 (`corpus/lessons/round-57.md` § Mistakes): the seat LedgerReplay (F1) found the awk form green on a missing object; captured by R30 in principle, not by the round-54 instrument. |
| R69 | A census's range citation resolves against a pinned file or a fixed revision, never against a live file whose length moves between regenerations: a range base that grows (a fleet log written on every tick) makes the census md change between two regenerations of the same tree, so the freshness verdict depends on when the gate runs rather than on the lane's content. The lane's tsv classification stays the stable record; the md records the moving EOF column. | M57-9 (`corpus/lessons/round-57.md` § Mistakes): `corpus/lessons/round-35.md:20` and `corpus/lessons/round-38.md:286` cite `.ufo/watchdog.log:1490` and `:1537`; the md EOF column moved 2080 → 2083 between two regenerations about four minutes apart while the tsv is byte-identical (`.ufo/waves/round-57/controls/census-live-log-diff-r57.txt`). Named open: the historical citations are append-only, and the census definition is an instrument change outside the frozen claims. |

## Round-58 additions — carry-in re-grep and anchor referents (append-only)

R6 form: every line above keeps its number; this block is appended after line 440 (R69). The file is 440 lines and its
sha256 before this append is `d6226b7e13e8607655f98e4dbafb783f04b8815e3db5f2c703e57a951cf55359`, and no earlier line is
edited. Each row closes a round-58 Mistakes row whose `captured-by` cell was `none` (R36): M58-1 and M58-3
(`corpus/lessons/round-58.md` § Mistakes).

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R70 | A carry-in status line in a round report (`standing`, `operator-side`, `unresolved`, `resolved`) is written only after the dependency it names is re-read in the bytes that carry it — the dispatched prompt, a receipt or a committed file — and the line cites the command and its count. A status that the bytes no longer support is a stale premise (R19). | M58-1 (`corpus/lessons/round-58.md` § Mistakes): the round-57 report line `.ufo/reports/round-57/kasegi.md:115` called DF-56-2 operator-side, while `grep -c 'at least 8 L2 subagents' .ufo/prompts/kasegi-round-57.md` prints 1. |
| R71 | Every free line anchor (`file:NN` or `file:NN-MM`) in a frontier entry, a SOURCES row or a lesson Gate names a line that carries the referent of the sentence it supports (the phrase or figure the sentence states). A pinned quote check (`source-recheck.py`) binds quotes at their anchor and does not bind free anchors, so the anchor-referent table — each cited line printed beside its sentence — is produced and read before the freeze and again before landing, and its output is cited in the lesson Gate. | M58-3 (`corpus/lessons/round-58.md` § Mistakes): the first F39 draft cited `S091.txt:12` for a sentence that sits on `:14`, and `S091.txt:29` for a sentence on `:30`; `source-recheck --source S091` passed the quotes. |

## Round-59 additions — derive-gate probe scope, two-open byte grading and gate docstrings (append-only)

R6 form: every line above keeps its number; this block is appended after line 452 (R71). Each row closes a round-59 Mistakes row whose `captured-by` cell was `none` (R36): M59-1 and M59-4 (R72), M59-7 (R73), M59-3 (R74). The rows that name an existing instrument or validator as catcher (M59-2, M59-5, M59-6) need no row.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R72 | A derive gate's claim is scoped to the probe classes its probe set measures: the probe set covers every metacharacter class of the shell grammar the gate names (separator, background, newline, command substitution, pipeline segment, redirect, short-circuit) before the claim is frozen, the residual classes are named in the claim and in the registry row, and the gate's quote model (escapes outside quotes) is stated. Red-before receipt: `grep -cE '^probe=P[1-8]-.* exit=[01] markers=1' .ufo/waves/round-59/controls/c1-probes-base-r59.txt` → 8 (every probe executes on the base gate). After-state: `grep -cE '^probe=P[1-8]-.* exit=2 markers=0' .ufo/waves/round-59/controls/c1-probes-lane-after-r59.txt` → 8. Residuals: `.ufo/waves/round-59/controls/c1-residual-lane-r59.txt` (B1-B4, B6 execute on the landed bytes; named open). | M59-1 (`corpus/lessons/round-59.md` § Mistakes): the base gate tested only the first `\|`-segment head, so eight of eight chaining probes executed; M59-4: an escaped quote outside quotes opens a quote in the scanner and not in bash, so B1 and B2 execute on the landed bytes. |
| R73 | Two opens of one endpoint that differ in raw bytes are graded on parsed structure, and the receipt names the transport difference (CRLF, serialization spacing); a raw byte-sha mismatch alone is not a content change. Check: `grep -c 'parsed-structure unified-diff lines (+/-): 0' .ufo/waves/round-59/controls/s092-variance-check.txt` → 1, and the raw-sha mismatch is recorded in the same receipt. | M59-7 (`corpus/lessons/round-59.md` § Mistakes): an independent curl open of the S092 endpoint differs from the instrument capture by raw sha (`.ufo/waves/round-59/controls/s092-variance-check.txt`, lengths 6365 and 6366 after CRLF normalization); the parsed structures are identical. |
| R74 | A gate's module docstring names the allow-list its code enforces; a change to the allow-list is not complete until the docstring names it. Check: `sed -n '1,40p' corpus/instruments/figure-audit.py \| grep -c 'allow-list:.*head'` → 0 at the round-59 tip (red: the docstring's allow-list sentence at line 27 does not name the `head ` and `tail ` entries added at `472737847`); the next instrument amendment must make it at least 1. The loose form `grep -c head` is not the check: it matches the docstring's "read-only head" phrase and reads 1. | M59-3 (`corpus/lessons/round-59.md` § Mistakes): the docstring's allow-list sentence was not updated when `head` and `tail` joined the allow-list at `472737847`; the frozen candidate's sha256 binds the bytes, so the amendment waits for a new freeze. |
| R75 | A negative probe of a bypass class is shown for every argument form the class admits (a single form is not a negative), and a registry row's safety or write-scope sentence is bound to a receipt for each class it names; the sentence may not exceed the receipts. Red-before: `grep -c 'markers=1' .ufo/waves/round-59/controls/c1-xclass-lane-r59.txt` reads at least 1 (X1 executes), while `grep -c 'B7-git-show-output.*markers=0' .ufo/waves/round-59/controls/c1-residual-lane-r59.txt` reads 1 (the single-form negative). | M59-8 (`corpus/lessons/round-59.md` § Mistakes): B7 was called "NOT a bypass" from `git show --output=MARK-B7 HEAD:README.md` (exit 128); `git show --output=MARK-X1 HEAD` creates the file (`controls/c1-xclass-lane-r59.txt`). M59-9: the SKILL registry sentence "writes nothing in the lane" was falsified by the X-series (`w1/ReviewerC1.md` §4). |
| R76 | An admitted entry is checked sentence by sentence against its pinned quotes (a claim table: GROUNDED, OVERREACH or UNGROUNDED) before the gate counts it; a sentence that names an attribute or characterization no quote carries is OVERREACH. Red-before: the pre-correction entry at `4727378` has three OVERREACH and one UNGROUNDED rows (`.ufo/waves/round-59/w1/EntryClaimTable.md`). | M59-10 (`corpus/lessons/round-59.md` § Mistakes): "the tracker's saved search for the project", "per-finding metadata contract", "coarse triage field", "the documents S010 already mines" and "kept in the tracker's Big Sleep component" were admitted without a claim table. |
| R77 | A frozen counting command runs literally from the lane root in a fresh clone; a command whose path the runner changes directory away from before use is a counter defect, recorded as such. Red-before: `.ufo/waves/round-59/w1/ExecCounters.md` (1 of 15 frozen commands fails its want, counter-side). | M59-11 (`corpus/lessons/round-59.md` § Mistakes): C1 counting command 1 uses a relative instrument path, and `controls/c1-probe-run.sh` changes directory into each probe root before running the instrument. |
| R78 | A frozen counting command's `want` is the value the command prints on the frozen tree, measured before the freeze — never a hand-typed want. Red-before: `.ufo/waves/round-60/claims.json` C2 counting command 8 (want 1, the implemented tree prints 2; superseded by `.ufo/waves/round-60/claims-erratum-1.md`). Check: rerun the command on the frozen tree and compare its printed value with the want. Grounded in M60-1. |
| R81 | A flag screen's probe set includes the token decodings the shell applies (quoted, backslash-escaped, ANSI-C, clustered short flags, abbreviated long options) and a comment-model probe; the screen's docstring may not claim more classes than the decoded spellings it refuses. Red-before: the round-60 wave admitted 24 write/execute derives (9 with the audit green) through quoted/clustered/abbreviated/ANSI-C spellings and a `#'` comment-phantom quote (`.ufo/waves/round-60/w1/CriticC1.md`, `w1/ExecEscapes.md`, `w1/ReviewerC1.md` §F1). Grounded in M60-9. |
| R82 | A committed harness fails closed (nonzero on a missing instrument or unusable scratch) and uses only its declared baseline tools; a selftest's case count is host-conditional unless the subprocess interpreter is the one running it. Red-before: `.ufo/waves/round-60/w1/ScoutHostBound.md` §5 (the selftest prints `cases=20 failures=20` on a python3-only host; the new runner returns 0 on every failure mode and needs GNU `realpath -m` and inetutils `hostname`). Grounded in M60-10. |

## Round-61 carry-in correction — missing R79/R80 (append-only)

The round-60 lesson and LOG claimed R79/R80 landed; the round-60 closure D1 found both absent. The rows below land those intended criteria now, after existing R81/R82; prior bytes and ids remain unchanged.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R79 | A write-class probe runs in the environment the write class needs, and its base receipt must show the write. A git write-flag probe needs a fixture whose toplevel is checked before any commit; a probe where the write cannot manifest is vacuous. | M60-3 in corpus/lessons/round-60.md; round-60 closure D1; the first Q6 probe had markers=0 outside a repository, the corrected base receipt has markers=1. |
| R80 | The extended pre-freeze screen and anchor-referent table carry a receipt timestamp before the freeze commit. A later screen is a safety net, not the promised pre-freeze screen; report the process miss rather than silently calling it conformant. | M60-6 in corpus/lessons/round-60.md; round-60 closure D1; round-60 neighbor-screen ran after 66e6241. Round-61 controls/neighbor-screen-r61.txt precedes 6f88f3a7. |
| R83 | A report's criterion-added list is checked against actual criterion rows in QUALITY.md, not the lesson's proposed text. Missing ids are unresolved until their rows land, and corrections append rather than rewriting the filed claim. | M61-1 in corpus/lessons/round-61.md (round-60 closure D1); controls/carry-in-r61.txt measures R79/R80 absent at base and present after repair. |

## Round-65 additions — measured base models, nav classification, one counter per claim and a declared base reference (append-only)

R6 form: every line above keeps its number; this block is appended after line 478. The file was 478 lines and
110820 bytes and its sha256 before this append is `6f1f227cf1c44d5fab8cbc1db9dd4c03d1f93a45b5a2b417d95cbd6fc85bfc66`.
Each row closes a round-65 Mistakes row (`corpus/lessons/round-65.md` § Mistakes) whose `captured-by` cell
named no criterion.

| id | criterion | grounded in |
|---|---|---|
| R84 | A base behaviour stated in a seat spec or claim draft is measured before the spec is dispatched: the seat's first base receipt is the record, and a spec sentence the receipt contradicts is corrected in the frozen predicate, not carried into it. Check: the receipt line that contradicts the spec is quoted beside the corrected predicate sentence. | M65-1: the r65 item text asserted the final-stage signal "reads ok at base" while `w1/ExecSignalProbes/probes-base.txt` printed `PROBE P3 mode=base audit_exit=1 verdict=stale reason=derive-exit--15`. The base defect there is the malformed label, not a false green. |
| R85 | A phrase-absence screen over captures classifies site navigation and boilerplate before it reports presence or absence: a hit in a shared nav block is not content evidence for the term it carries. Check: every downstream-capture hit is printed with its line and adjudicated in the same receipt. | M65-4: `controls/prefreeze-screen-r65.txt` first reported three capture hits for "PSIRT Services Framework"; all three are FIRST site navigation at line 201 of S001, S002 and S053. |
| R86 | A claim names exactly one counter; a second independently authored driver is corroborating predata, never a second counter for the same claim, and the report says which artifact is the counter. | M65-6: seat ExecDriver produced `w1/ExecDriver/verify-r65.py` (base `failures=6` on its own enumeration) while the L1 froze a different 8-measurement driver; only the frozen one backs C1. |
| R87 | A committed-input driver declares, in its own docstring, which revision supplies the base state and which pins apply in which mode; a pin set that names candidate bytes does not count against a base trial. Check: run the driver at the base revision and at the measured revision and compare the `input-hashes` note (`gate-pin=base-tree` versus `gate-pin=candidate`). | M65-2 and M65-3: the first final run read the landed gate as the base (`controls/final-verification-pre-erratum-r65.txt`, 8/2) and a base trial counted the candidate pin as a fourth failure; erratum 1 fixed both and the receipts are `controls/driver-base-donothing-finaldrv-r65.txt` (8/3) and `controls/final-verification-r65.txt` (8/0). |
| R88 | An exemption class in a gate is probed for the error it can mask, not only for the behaviour it permits: a rule that treats one status as benign names the error classes it can hide, and the probe set carries at least one control where that status hides a real failure. | M65-7 (`corpus/lessons/round-65.md`): the SIGPIPE exemption keeps `cat big.txt missing.txt | head -n 1` green while `cat missing.txt | wc -l` is stale (`.ufo/waves/round-65/w1/CriticC1.md` § H1; receipts `q2-missing-input-contrast.txt`, `q2-sigpipe-masks-error-native.txt`). |
| R89 | A driver that pins inputs reads those inputs from a revision, never from the mutable worktree; its scratch root is guarded and every child has a timeout. Check: measurement 8 hashes revision bytes, a `--work .` run refuses, and no measurement child can hang unbounded. | M65-8 and the sentinel's S-02/S-03/S-08: the driver read the probe/battery/candidate from the worktree and hashed worktree bytes, `--work` was unguarded and children had no timeout (`.ufo/waves/round-65/w1/CriticC1.md` § D2, `w1/SentinelScope.md`). Fixed in erratum 2 (`155737d0`), which re-measured 8/3 before and 8/0 after. |
| R90 | A capture's declared body range is read from the document's own structure — first body heading to the last annex/glossary line before site navigation resumes — and a range that ends inside an annex is a defect. Check: the boundary lines are printed in the Gate and the range's last line is not an annex heading. | M65-9 (`corpus/lessons/round-65.md`): the round-65 manifest declared `body_lines [216,1450]`, stopping inside Annex 2 and excluding the Glossary (L1498-1536, maturity definition L1510) while navigation resumes at L1537 (`.ufo/waves/round-65/w1/EntryS098/gate.md` § Finding for the L1). |
| R91 | An entry edited after its sentence table was produced is re-audited sentence by sentence before the commit: a negative appended later ("states no …", "has no …") is checked against the capture with the command that would falsify it, and the cited measurement must support every negative in the sentence it accompanies. | M65-10 (`corpus/lessons/round-65.md`): the L1's maturity bullet said "only maturity statement" and "no assessment method" while L675 states a measurement method; the cited `grep -cE 'Maturity Level|Level [123]'` = 0 supported only the level negatives (`.ufo/waves/round-65/w1/EntryS098.md`). |

